  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "CIN-01",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "Why two arithmetic vertices generate a dense archimedean subgroup",
      "anchor": "inputs-common-transvections",
      "statement": "For a totally complex number field L, prime π in 𝒪_L and N≥3, the simultaneous archimedean image of ⟨K₀,K₁⟩ is dense in ∏_w SL_N(ℂ).",
      "hypotheses": "N≥3 ensures a third index in the commutator argument. The scalar-density lemma uses only π≠0 and π not a unit and total complexity. The accompanying flag bound additionally requires |𝒪_L/(π)|≥25.",
      "definitions": [
        "D_r=diag(π,…,π,1,…,1) with the first r entries π, K_r=D_r⁻¹SL_N(𝒪_L)D_r, j(g)=(w(g))_w.",
        "T_ij(a)=I+a e_ij. The group contains T_ij(t/π^m) for all i≠j, all integral t, and all m≥0.",
        "Subgroup here means the group generated by the first two vertices; it becomes the product of all complex factors after taking the ordinary topological closure."
      ],
