      "id": "CIN-03",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "Constancy in Haar measure and the zero-mean complement",
      "anchor": "inputs-common-null",
      "statement": "The common invariant classes are almost everywhere constant. The zero-integral average is orthogonal to them on the finite common cover. A mean-zero invariant class itself must be zero because the volume of that cover is positive and finite.",
      "hypotheses": "Second countable locally compact Hausdorff topological group with Borel Haar measure, measurable real-valued representative, density, a finite positive-volume common fundamental domain, and zero integral of the selected average.",
      "definitions": [
        "For Borel U⊆ℝ, T=F⁻¹(U). The a.e. stabilizer of T consists of g whose left-translation preimage of T differs from it by a Haar-null set.",
        "The compact-localized symmetric difference is continuous in measure in the translation. Ergodicity of the left regular action is a separate pinned Mathlib result.",
        "If F=c almost everywhere, ⟨F,v⟩_DB=c[B-index]∫_D₀v=0, with [B-index]=[K₀:B]."
      ],
